# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MailySend
#
# Intentionally empty of keys, and that is the whole point of the file.
#
# This is what the "Deploy to Cloudflare" flow reads to build its variables
# form, and that form behaves in a way worth writing down: it shows key names
# only — never these comments — and it stores every answer as a *secret*, so
# each field renders as a blank, masked, mandatory-looking box. It does not
# prefill from the values here. A key with a perfectly good default in this
# file is therefore indistinguishable, on the form, from a credential the
# deployment cannot start without.
#
# So every key lives in docs/CONFIGURATION.md instead, where each one is listed
# with its default and what changing it does, and is set after the fact with
# `wrangler secret put` or a `vars` entry. Nothing is required: the instance
# generates its signing secret, learns its own public URL from the first real
# request, applies its migrations, creates the workspace, and prints a
# bootstrap API key to the log.
#
# MS_MODE, MS_LANDING, MS_DEFAULT_PROVIDER and EVENT_DETAIL default in code to
# `single`, `app`, `cloudflare` and on. MS_OWNER_EMAIL, MS_REQUIRE_CLAIM_CODE
# and the MS_OIDC_* group are optional and documented on the same page; /setup
# asks the ownership question itself, and by default the first person to reach
# it takes the instance. The other two close that window: MS_OWNER_EMAIL
# reserves the claim for one address, MS_REQUIRE_CLAIM_CODE=1 mints a code on
# first boot that /setup then asks for.
#
# Transport credentials are never here: they are configured at
# Settings -> Transports, encrypted with AES-GCM at rest, and never returned by
# the API. See docs/SENDING.md for what each transport needs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
